From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Author: Jeremiah Jordan
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 18:39:37 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Transparent data encryption: key providers whose keys are
 authorized for a limited time

A KeyProvider may now say that its keys must not be cached (keysMayBeCached, default true), and
resolve a new file's key and the alias its header records in one lookup (keyForNewFile). For such
a provider CipherFactory keeps neither keys nor ciphers between files, and destroys each key once
its cipher is initialized. A file still being appended to (a commit log segment) asks for its key
again before each section it encrypts (keyForAppend, EncryptionContext.reauthorize), with the IV
its header records, so a key whose authorization ends stops that file's writes, not only the next
file's. A section with nothing to encrypt (the empty closing section at shutdown) asks for no key.
Header aliases read back from files share the node's key provider instead of loading a new one.
Providers that keep the defaults (JKSKeyProvider) are unchanged.
